1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,144
Lesson 3.3.7: Hospitalizations, Referrals and Escalation.

2
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,768
This lesson covers the last four sections of the problem note.

3
00:00:13,818 --> 00:00:17,536
Hospitalizations and ER visits, found in the census,

4
00:00:17,576 --> 00:00:20,813
the transfer forms, and the progress notes.

5
00:00:20,863 --> 00:00:25,365
New referrals related to the condition, found in the patient's orders.

6
00:00:25,415 --> 00:00:29,743
Medication changes, which only apply to follow-up patients.

7
00:00:29,793 --> 00:00:35,109
And escalation: drug-drug interactions, and the red flags in the care policy.

8
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:41,139
Four sections remain: hospitalizations or ER visits,

9
00:00:41,179 --> 00:00:45,224
new referrals, medication changes, and escalation.

10
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,549
Hospitalizations follow the same window as provider visits:

11
00:00:53,589 --> 00:00:56,701
for a scratch patient, the past three months.

12
00:00:57,667 --> 00:01:00,822
And every hospitalization or ER visit counts,

13
00:01:00,862 --> 00:01:04,207
whether it's related to the condition or not.

14
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:10,213
Three places can show one: the census, the assessments, and the progress notes.

15
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:16,413
The Census tab lists the patient's status over time.

16
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:23,142
The current status shows the patient active since July 1.

17
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:26,746
An earlier status like this one, on June 10,

18
00:01:26,786 --> 00:01:31,120
records a hospital stay of less than 24 hours.

19
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:35,996
Inside our window, it would count as a hospitalization.

20
00:01:37,167 --> 00:01:40,009
But three months back only reaches August 25,

21
00:01:40,049 --> 00:01:44,742
and there's no hospital or ER status after July 1.

22
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:48,906
To confirm, we check the assessments next.

23
00:01:50,567 --> 00:01:53,242
With View All and the status on All, as before,

24
00:01:53,282 --> 00:01:57,731
a search for transfer finds any transfer outside the facility.

25
00:02:01,967 --> 00:02:05,440
A lift transfer evaluation isn't what we're after.

26
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:12,208
What we want is a form like this one: the eINTERACT transfer form,

27
00:02:12,248 --> 00:02:16,384
the record of a transfer to a hospital or ER.

28
00:02:16,434 --> 00:02:21,764
This one is dated June 10, the same day as the hospital stay in the census.

29
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:27,173
Printing it shows where the patient was sent, when, and why.

30
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:35,075
Its transfer details show the patient sent to Frisbie Memorial Hospital on February 26,

31
00:02:35,115 --> 00:02:36,479
because of a fall.

32
00:02:36,533 --> 00:02:39,213
Had it been inside our window, we'd document it.

33
00:02:39,253 --> 00:02:41,988
It's well outside, so it's left out.

34
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:45,114
The progress notes are the last place,

35
00:02:45,154 --> 00:02:47,965
already filtered to the past three months.

36
00:02:48,015 --> 00:02:51,847
A search for hospitalization finds only a screening answer:

37
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:56,102
recent hospitalization in the last 30 days, no.

38
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,213
And a search for emergency shows no emergency department visit

39
00:03:00,253 --> 00:03:02,146
or transfer either.

40
00:03:04,433 --> 00:03:09,416
So the note says: no hospitalizations or ER visits this cycle.

41
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:10,422
Had there been one,

42
00:03:10,462 --> 00:03:14,547
the note would say when the patient was hospitalized, and why.

43
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:19,555
Next, new referrals related to the condition:

44
00:03:19,595 --> 00:03:24,354
the patient referred to an outside provider for hyperlipidemia.

45
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:29,567
That means a referral or an upcoming appointment with a cardiologist,

46
00:03:29,607 --> 00:03:34,085
an internal medicine specialist, or a family medicine doctor.

47
00:03:35,233 --> 00:03:38,675
For a scratch patient, we look for upcoming appointments.

48
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:44,967
In the Orders tab, the Other category lists the patient's appointments.

49
00:03:46,733 --> 00:03:50,542
Sorting by start date brings the upcoming ones to the top.

50
00:03:51,733 --> 00:03:55,981
Here, the only upcoming order is a lab order, not an appointment.

51
00:03:57,633 --> 00:04:02,008
An appointment looks like this one: follow up as needed with dermatology,

52
00:04:02,047 --> 00:04:06,142
optometry, audiology, dental, and podiatry.

53
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:13,785
An upcoming appointment with a specialty relevant to the condition is documented as a new referral.

54
00:04:13,825 --> 00:04:15,731
Here, there's none.

55
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:22,727
Medication changes compare today's medications for the condition

56
00:04:22,767 --> 00:04:25,756
with those on the last date of service.

57
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:30,885
A scratch patient has no last date of service yet,

58
00:04:30,925 --> 00:04:34,711
so the note simply says: no medication changes.

59
00:04:36,433 --> 00:04:40,078
For a follow-up patient, the Orders tab, filtered to pharmacy,

60
00:04:40,118 --> 00:04:46,768
shows today's medication: rosuvastatin calcium 20 mg, once daily.

61
00:04:50,167 --> 00:04:52,649
And the Order Summary report, under Reports,

62
00:04:52,689 --> 00:04:56,124
shows the medications on the last date of service.

63
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:04,270
Filtered to the pharmacy category, with active orders as of that date,

64
00:05:07,067 --> 00:05:09,245
for example, October 20.

65
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:14,868
Running the report lists every medication active on that day.

66
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:22,947
Any change in the medications for the condition goes into the medication changes section:

67
00:05:22,987 --> 00:05:28,400
a dose increased or decreased, a medication switched, stopped, or added.

68
00:05:32,367 --> 00:05:36,758
The last section is escalation, and only two things are escalated.

69
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:42,883
First, drug-drug interactions involving the medications for our condition.

70
00:05:43,933 --> 00:05:44,526
Second,

71
00:05:44,566 --> 00:05:50,165
the grounds for escalation listed in the Escalation Guide tab of the care policy.

72
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:57,099
For hyperlipidemia: worsening chest pain, shortness of breath, changes in vision,

73
00:05:57,139 --> 00:06:03,970
severe abdominal pain, a recent heart attack or stroke, or severe pancreatitis.

74
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:10,562
Drug-drug interactions show up in the orders, as a flag next to the medication.

75
00:06:14,333 --> 00:06:16,703
Here, flags mark a dose warning for senna,

76
00:06:16,743 --> 00:06:21,117
and an allergy warning and a black box warning for lamotrigine.

77
00:06:26,533 --> 00:06:30,904
Our CCM medication, rosuvastatin, has no flags at all,

78
00:06:30,944 --> 00:06:33,905
so there's no interaction to escalate.

79
00:06:35,033 --> 00:06:39,388
The other grounds are searched in the progress notes, just like the symptoms.

80
00:06:41,567 --> 00:06:45,722
With nothing found, the note says: no escalations this cycle.

81
00:06:47,533 --> 00:06:50,177
And with that, the problem note is complete.

82
00:06:52,900 --> 00:07:00,341
The CCM Clinical Tool adds the patient education for the condition at the end of the note automatically.

83
00:07:05,733 --> 00:07:09,215
Two documentation standards must always close the note:

84
00:07:09,255 --> 00:07:13,153
the patient education section, and the care plan template.

85
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:19,876
Before saving, we read the whole note for missing information or inaccuracies,

86
00:07:19,916 --> 00:07:22,337
especially in the gaps in care.

87
00:07:26,133 --> 00:07:31,049
This line, for example, carries a time frame and a reference range we don't need.

88
00:07:31,089 --> 00:07:36,078
Blood pressure is uncontrolled says it all, so the rest is erased.

89
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:40,779
Then we copy the note and keep it, in a sticky note for example,

90
00:07:40,819 --> 00:07:44,450
until we add it to the problem note in ThoroughCare.

91
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:52,928
To wrap up: hospitalizations and ER visits come from the census,

92
00:07:52,968 --> 00:07:56,240
the transfer forms, and the progress notes.

93
00:07:56,290 --> 00:08:00,136
New referrals are the upcoming appointments related to the condition,

94
00:08:00,176 --> 00:08:01,841
in the Other orders.

95
00:08:01,891 --> 00:08:06,911
Medication changes compare today's medications with the last date of service,

96
00:08:06,951 --> 00:08:09,874
so they only apply to follow-up patients.

97
00:08:09,924 --> 00:08:12,361
Escalation covers drug-drug interactions

98
00:08:12,401 --> 00:08:15,750
and the red flags in the Escalation Guide.

99
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:20,116
And every note ends with the patient education and the care plan template,

100
00:08:20,156 --> 00:08:22,147
checked before it's saved.

101
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:27,635
The problem note is now ready to add to ThoroughCare.
